<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <!-- THIS GRANT IS CORRECT AND MUST NOT BE REMOVED.
         The Flutter tool needs android.permission.INTERNET to reach the Dart VM
         service in the running app: breakpoints, hot reload and DevTools all go
         over it. It applies to the debug build only and never ships.

         Removing it does not improve the offline guarantee by one byte — the
         shipping manifests in main/ and release/ are what the user installs — and
         it does break `flutter run`. catchlaw-offline-guarantee rule 4 states the
         consequence plainly: a guard that breaks flutter run is a guard someone
         deletes before lunch, and then the real guarantee goes with it.

         app/test/policy/android_manifest_test.dart ASSERTS this line is present,
         which reads backwards until you have read the reason above. The guard test
         and check_no_network.sh both skip this source set for the same reason.

         CONFIRMED ON HARDWARE, once, at the E01 close-out (E01/T06 definition of
         done). Pixel 8a emulator, API 35, debug build:
           adb shell dumpsys package app.catchlaw.catchlaw
             requested permissions: android.permission.INTERNET
             install permissions:   android.permission.INTERNET: granted=true
           flutter run  ->  Reloaded 1 of 1190 libraries
                            Restarted application in 523ms
         An edited widget reached the running app, so this grant is not a
         theoretical requirement copied from the Flutter template — it is load
         bearing, and the release AAB is separately proved to lack it. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET"/>
</manifest>
